parts.text:
Now I have the definitive answer. The shared file loader `ks` in p5.js v2.3.1 is:

```js
async function ks(e, t) {
  try {
    const r = await fetch(e);
    if (r.ok) {
      let e;
      switch (t) {
        case "json": e = await r.json(); break;
        case "text": e = await r.text(); break;
        case "arrayBuffer": e = await r.arrayBuffer(); break;
        case "blob": e = await r.blob(); break;
        case "bytes": ...
        default: throw new Error("Unsupported response type")
      }
      return { data: e, headers: r.headers }
    }
    ...
  }
}
```

It uses `fetch(e)` where `e` is the path/URL. The `fetch` API in the WebView **DOES support `data:` URIs**! So:
- `loadModel('/assets/teapot.obj')` → `fetch('/assets/teapot.obj')` → fails (relative path, no server)
- `loadModel('data:application/octet-stream;base64,...')` → `fetch('data:...')` → **WORKS** in modern WebViews (fetch supports data: URLs)
- `loadModel('https://p5js.org/assets/teapot.obj')` → `fetch('https://...')` → works IF network is available AND CORS permits

This confirms:
- **Option A (data URIs)** is fully feasible for ALL loaders (loadModel, loadImage, loadShader, loadFont, loadJSON, loadStrings, loadBytes, loadTable) because they all route through `fetch()` which supports `data:` URIs. The io.yaml course already proves this works for loadImage/loadJSON/loadStrings in this exact WebView.
- **Option C (absolute p5 CDN URLs)** is feasible IF the WebView has network access AND p5js.org/CORS allows it. p5js.org serves assets with permissive CORS. But it requires runtime network, which defeats the "fully offline bundled" design.
